Subject: [PATCH] request scratch backing from the guest runtime

A dispatch that needs private memory hangs the guest on the vfio-user path.
init_wavefront_regs() probes the per-wave scratch slice, the probe comes back
Faulted, and the CP returns Faulted at command_processor.cpp:726-728 without
ever dispatching the wave. HIP sees a dispatch that never retires.

Instrumenting the CP shows why. The queue's amd_queue_t is being read
correctly -- read_dispatch_id_field_base_byte_offset reads back as 128, exactly
the offset the CP subtracted to find the struct, and the scratch SRD carries
ROCr's format and swizzle bits -- but every scratch field in it is empty:

  compute_tmpring_size          0
  scratch_resource_descriptor   base 0, num_records 0 (config dwords set)
  scratch_backing_memory_location   0
  scratch_backing_memory_byte_size  0
  alt_scratch_*                 0
  queue_inactive_signal         non-null

So the guest runtime published no scratch, and the CP fell back to the
hardcoded 0x1_0000_0000 pool at :658-661, which nothing on this path ever maps.
Even a single byte there faults.

That is not a guest bug. gfx12 ROCr allocates scratch on demand, not at queue
creation: it leaves the fields zero and waits for the packet processor to ask
through the queue-inactive signal. AqlQueue::DynamicQueueEventsHandler gates on
`error_code & 0x401`, scans the ring from read_dispatch_id for the first packet
declaring a private segment, sizes a pool for it, fills the queue's scratch
fields in, clears the signal and re-rings the doorbell. The non-null
queue_inactive_signal above is the runtime saying it is listening. rocJITsu
never asks, so the fields stay zero forever.

The emulator cannot paper over this by allocating the pool itself. On this path
snapshot_gpu_access() walks the guest's live amdgpu page tables and the CP is
only a reader of them -- there is no map API, and a fabricated base would have
to be backed by forging PTEs behind amdgpu's back. The scratch_allocator_ and
scratch_resolver_ hooks that would otherwise repair this are installed only
from kmd/linux/simulated_kfd.cpp, so they are dead code here; the instrumented
run confirms both are empty.

So: ask. When the queue publishes a zero backing location and no resolver is
installed, store the insufficient-scratch code into the queue-inactive signal,
poke the event mailbox, raise the interrupt, and return Blocked so the packet
stays pending. ROCr's handler needs to find the dispatch still sitting at
read_dispatch_id, so retiring it would break the handshake. When admission
retries, the location is real and the existing probe passes on its own merits.

Bit 10 of the code selects the lane count ROCr sizes the pool for, so it is set
from the dispatch's own wave size rather than assumed.

A zero backing is not the only shortfall, because the runtime answers the
question it was asked. It sizes the pool for the dispatch it found in the ring
and no larger, so the next dispatch wanting more per lane runs off the end of a
pool that is, by every field it can see, present and valid. A reproducer that
asks for 288 bytes of private segment and then 1056 shows it exactly: the
second dispatch gets a mapped pool base and an unmapped wave slice, because
ROCr published compute_tmpring_size 0x24200 -- 512 waves per shader engine at
36 granules of 256 bytes, so 9216 bytes per wave over a 9 MiB pool -- while
slot 512 of a 33792-byte stride lands 17.3 MiB in.

So the size is asked for on the same terms as the location. The comparison is
made against scratch_wave64_lane_byte_size rather than by decoding
compute_tmpring_size, because that field times 64 is the published per-wave
stride by construction, with no granule or shader-engine count to assume (144 *
64 = 9216 on the run above, exactly). Both conditions raise the same request
over the same signal, since to the runtime they are the same event.

The probe at :726-728 is left exactly as it is. It is the only bound anywhere
between a fabricated scratch base and guest memory -- nothing reads
scratch_backing_memory_byte_size and the CDNA5 address calculation applies no
limit -- so relaxing it would turn a loud hang into silent corruption. The
point of this change is that the probe now has real backing to pass against.

Three guards on the request itself. If the queue has no usable inactive signal
there is nobody to ask, so the old Faulted result stands, with a log line
saying so instead of a bare probe refusal. If the packet declares no private
segment -- only the kernel descriptor does -- the request is skipped, because
ROCr keys its ring scan on the packet's private_segment_size and would find
nothing to size, leaving a raised code no retry could clear. And the queue
records the largest per-wave size it has asked for, so a request answered with
less than it asked for is not made twice: the runtime has given what it can,
and the probe reports the shortfall rather than the two sides trading requests
forever. What counts as answered has to be chosen carefully. Most of the
retries that follow a request arrive while the runtime is still working, and
the clear of the inactive signal is observable before the published fields
land, so a guard that reads only the signal abandons requests that were about
to be answered -- which it did, intermittently, until the witness below was
added. So the queue also records the geometry it was publishing when the
request went out, and only a geometry that has since moved counts as an
answer. The fields do not land together either, so a retry that sees the new
stride against a location still reading zero waits as well rather than calling
that a shortfall. The guard matters at all because the emulator rounds its
per-wave stride up to 1024 bytes while the runtime's gfx12 granule is 256, so
there are sizes the runtime cannot satisfy exactly. Everything after the
compare-exchange either completes or rolls the signal back, for the same
reason.

One answer is not a shortfall but a refusal. If the runtime cannot acquire a
pool at all it returns without publishing, and its handler then suspends the
queue and stores -1 into the inactive signal. That is read as a refusal and
faulted on, because waiting for an answer that is never coming is a hang whose
cause is already known.

Not every refusal arrives that way, though, and the largest dispatch in the
reproducer shows the gap. 10272 bytes of private segment over 1024 slots is
336 MB against a guest GPU given 256, and the request for it is raised once
and then never answered: no publication, no sign bit, and no diagnostic from
the runtime, for as long as the dispatch is left waiting. A request the
runtime drops in silence therefore still hangs the queue, which needs a bound
on how long it will wait to be answered. That bound is not in this change.

Not fixed here, and worth separating. The emulator computes the wave slice with
its own 1024-byte-rounded stride instead of the stride the runtime published,
so the two agree only when the requirement is already 1024-aligned. And the
per-CP scratch geometry is never configured on this path (scratch_xcc_count_
stays 1, scratch_wave_divisor_ stays 1 while scratch_waves_per_se_ is 1024), so
slot indices are computed against 16384 slots while the guest is told the
device has 1024. Both are latent for small dispatches and need their own
change.

--- a/emulation/rocjitsu/lib/rocjitsu/src/rocjitsu/vm/amdgpu/command_processor.cpp
+++ b/emulation/rocjitsu/lib/rocjitsu/src/rocjitsu/vm/amdgpu/command_processor.cpp
@@ -242,6 +242,148 @@
   return {.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Malformed};
 }
 
+// gfx12 ROCr does not allocate a scratch pool when it creates a queue. It
+// publishes amd_queue_t with a zero scratch_backing_memory_location, a zero-base
+// scratch SRD and a zero compute_tmpring_size, then waits to be asked: the
+// packet processor stores an insufficient-scratch code into the queue-inactive
+// signal, and AqlQueue::DynamicScratchHandler allocates a pool sized for the
+// dispatch it finds at read_dispatch_id, fills the queue's scratch fields in,
+// clears the signal and re-rings the doorbell.
+//
+// Bit 0 is the request itself; bit 10 tells ROCr the dispatch is wave32 so it
+// sizes the pool per 32 lanes rather than 64. Both are read straight out of the
+// runtime: amd_aql_queue.cpp gates on `error_code & 0x401` and derives
+// `lanes_per_wave = (error_code & 0x400) ? 32 : 64`.
+constexpr uint64_t kScratchRequestInsufficient = 0x1;
+constexpr uint64_t kScratchRequestWave32 = 0x400;
+
+// amd_signal_t places the value at +8, the event mailbox pointer at +16 and the
+// event id at +24. Same layout CompletionTracker uses to publish queue-idle.
+constexpr uint64_t kSignalValueOffset = 8;
+// AqlQueue's error handler stores -1 into the inactive signal on its way out.
+constexpr uint64_t kSignalTerminated = 0x8000000000000000ULL;
+constexpr uint64_t kSignalMailboxPointerOffset = 16;
+constexpr uint64_t kSignalEventIdOffset = 24;
+
+/// @brief Ask the guest runtime to back this queue's scratch.
+///
+/// @details Returns Blocked on success rather than retiring the packet: ROCr's
+/// handler scans the ring from read_dispatch_id for the first dispatch that
+/// declares a private segment, so the packet has to still be pending when it
+/// runs. Blocked is also the answer while a request is outstanding --
+/// re-interrupting a handler that is already working would race it.
+///
+/// Returns nullopt for the one case that is not worth asking about: the runtime
+/// has already answered a request for this much and published less. Admission
+/// then continues and the per-wave probe reports the shortfall, which is a
+/// better failure than the two sides trading requests forever.
+///
+/// The queue's request bookkeeping is advanced here, at the point the request
+/// actually goes out.
+std::optional<AqlAdmissionResult> request_queue_scratch(const GpuVmAccess &access,
+                                                        uint64_t queue_ptr, AqlQueueRecord &queue,
+                                                        bool wave32, uint64_t required_per_wave,
+                                                        uint64_t published_per_wave) {
+  const AtomicLoadResult handle = access.atomic_load(
+      queue_ptr + offsetof(amd_queue_t, queue_inactive_signal), sizeof(uint64_t));
+  if (handle.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
+    return admission_from_vm_outcome(handle.outcome);
+  // A 64-byte-aligned non-null handle is the only shape amd_signal_t takes;
+  // anything else means the field is not a signal and cannot carry the request.
+  if (handle.value == 0 || (handle.value & 0x3f) != 0) {
+    util::Logger::warn(std::format(
+        "cp: queue {:#x} published no scratch backing and has no usable queue-inactive "
+        "signal ({:#x}); the dispatch cannot be backed",
+        queue_ptr, handle.value));
+    return AqlAdmissionResult{.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Faulted,
+                              .diagnostic = AqlPacketDiagnostic::InvalidSignalAddress};
+  }
+
+  const AtomicLoadResult pending =
+      access.atomic_load(handle.value + kSignalValueOffset, sizeof(uint64_t));
+  if (pending.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
+    return admission_from_vm_outcome(pending.outcome);
+
+  // The runtime writes the sign bit into the signal when it gives up on the
+  // queue: HandleInsufficientScratch returns without publishing if it cannot
+  // acquire a pool, and the handler then suspends the queue and stores -1.
+  // Waiting on that is a hang with a known cause, so report it instead.
+  if ((pending.value & kSignalTerminated) != 0) {
+    util::Logger::warn(std::format(
+        "cp: queue {:#x} asked the runtime for {} bytes of scratch per wave and the runtime "
+        "gave up on the queue (inactive signal now {:#x}); the device cannot back this dispatch",
+        queue_ptr, required_per_wave, pending.value));
+    return AqlAdmissionResult{.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Faulted,
+                              .diagnostic = AqlPacketDiagnostic::ScratchUnavailable};
+  }
+
+  // Any other non-zero value is a request still being answered.
+  if (pending.value != 0)
+    return AqlAdmissionResult{.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Blocked};
+
+  // A clear signal is not on its own proof that the runtime has answered: the
+  // CP can observe the clear before the published fields land. Published
+  // geometry that has moved since the request is proof, and if it moved and is
+  // still short then the runtime has given what it can.
+  if (queue.requested_scratch_per_wave >= required_per_wave) {
+    // Nothing has moved yet, or the stride has arrived and the location has
+    // not. The runtime publishes the geometry field by field, so a retry that
+    // catches it mid-publish has to wait rather than judge it.
+    if (published_per_wave == queue.scratch_published_at_request ||
+        published_per_wave >= required_per_wave)
+      return AqlAdmissionResult{.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Blocked};
+    util::Logger::warn(std::format(
+        "cp: queue {:#x} answered a request for {} bytes of scratch per wave with {}; "
+        "not asking again",
+        queue_ptr, required_per_wave, published_per_wave));
+    return std::nullopt;
+  }
+
+  // Read the mailbox before raising the request. Everything after the
+  // compare-exchange has to either complete or be rolled back, because a raised
+  // code that never reaches the runtime is a hang the retry cannot clear.
+  const AtomicLoadResult mailbox =
+      access.atomic_load(handle.value + kSignalMailboxPointerOffset, sizeof(uint64_t));
+  if (mailbox.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
+    return admission_from_vm_outcome(mailbox.outcome);
+  const AtomicLoadResult event = access.atomic_load(handle.value + kSignalEventIdOffset, 4);
+  if (event.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
+    return admission_from_vm_outcome(event.outcome);
+  const auto event_id = static_cast<uint32_t>(event.value);
+
+  const uint64_t error_code =
+      kScratchRequestInsufficient | (wave32 ? kScratchRequestWave32 : uint64_t{0});
+  const AtomicCompareExchangeResult raised =
+      access.compare_exchange(handle.value + kSignalValueOffset, sizeof(uint64_t), 0, error_code);
+  if (raised.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
+    return admission_from_vm_outcome(raised.outcome);
+  if (!raised.exchanged)
+    return AqlAdmissionResult{.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Blocked};
+  queue.requested_scratch_per_wave = required_per_wave;
+  queue.scratch_published_at_request = published_per_wave;
+
+  if (mailbox.value != 0) {
+    const VmAccessOutcome stored =
+        access.atomic_store(mailbox.value, sizeof(uint64_t), uint64_t{event_id});
+    if (stored != VmAccessOutcome::Complete) {
+      const AtomicCompareExchangeResult cleared = access.compare_exchange(
+          handle.value + kSignalValueOffset, sizeof(uint64_t), error_code, 0);
+      static_cast<void>(cleared);
+      return admission_from_vm_outcome(stored);
+    }
+  }
+
+  util::Logger::warn(std::format(
+      "cp: queue {:#x} needs {} bytes of {} scratch per wave; requesting it from the runtime "
+      "(inactive signal {:#x}, event {})",
+      queue_ptr, required_per_wave, wave32 ? "wave32" : "wave64", handle.value, event_id));
+
+  if (event_id != 0)
+    queue.interrupt_sink.deliver(queue.process_id, event_id);
+  queue.interrupt_sink.deliver(queue.process_id, 0);
+  return AqlAdmissionResult{.status = AqlAdmissionStatus::Blocked};
+}
+
 std::optional<AqlPacketDiagnostic> validate_cluster_shape(const DispatchEntry &dp) {
   if (!dp.has_workgroup_clusters())
     return std::nullopt;
@@ -2781,6 +2923,41 @@
       if (loaded.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
         return admission_from_vm_outcome(loaded.outcome);
       scratch_backing_addr = loaded.value;
+
+      // The runtime states its per-slot stride as a wave64 lane size, so the
+      // pool it published covers lane_size * 64 bytes per wave. It sizes that
+      // for the dispatch it was last asked about, which means a later, hungrier
+      // dispatch overruns the pool exactly the way a zero backing does, only
+      // further in. Both are the same conversation, so both ask.
+      const AtomicLoadResult lane = read_gpu_u64(
+          transaction_access, queue_ptr + offsetof(amd_queue_t, scratch_wave64_lane_byte_size));
+      if (lane.outcome != VmAccessOutcome::Complete)
+        return admission_from_vm_outcome(lane.outcome);
+      const uint64_t published_per_wave = (lane.value & 0xffffffffULL) * 64;
+      const uint64_t required_per_wave =
+          static_cast<uint64_t>(private_segment_fixed_size) * wave_size;
+
+      // A zero location is not an error on this path, it is the runtime waiting
+      // to be asked. scratch_resolver_ is the SimulatedKfd answer to the same
+      // question (SET_SCRATCH_BACKING_VA), so defer to it where it is installed;
+      // with a real guest runtime there is nothing to defer to and the request
+      // has to go out over the queue-inactive signal.
+      if (!scratch_resolver_ &&
+          (scratch_backing_addr == 0 || published_per_wave < required_per_wave)) {
+        if (pkt.private_segment_size == 0) {
+          // ROCr keys its ring scan on the packet's own private_segment_size,
+          // not on the kernel descriptor, so a request raised for a packet that
+          // declares zero would find no dispatch to size and never be answered.
+          util::Logger::warn(std::format(
+              "cp: queue {:#x} needs {} bytes of scratch per wave but the packet declares no "
+              "private segment (kernel descriptor asks for {} bytes); cannot request a pool",
+              queue_ptr, required_per_wave, kd.private_segment_fixed_size));
+        } else if (const std::optional<AqlAdmissionResult> requested =
+                       request_queue_scratch(transaction_access, queue_ptr, queue, wave_size == 32,
+                                             required_per_wave, published_per_wave)) {
+          return *requested;
+        }
+      }
     }
   }
 
--- a/emulation/rocjitsu/lib/rocjitsu/src/rocjitsu/vm/amdgpu/dispatch_entry.h
+++ b/emulation/rocjitsu/lib/rocjitsu/src/rocjitsu/vm/amdgpu/dispatch_entry.h
@@ -616,6 +616,19 @@
   /// that owns the queue.
   bool fanout_replica = false;
   Status status = Status::Idle;
+  /// Largest per-wave scratch, in bytes, this CP has asked the guest runtime to
+  /// back on this queue. gfx12 runtimes publish no scratch until the packet
+  /// processor requests it, and size the pool for the dispatch they were asked
+  /// about, so a hungrier dispatch has to ask again. Recording the high-water
+  /// mark keeps that from becoming a trade: if the runtime answers a request
+  /// with less than it was asked for, it has given what it can and asking a
+  /// second time would only spin.
+  uint64_t requested_scratch_per_wave = 0;
+  /// Per-wave scratch the queue was publishing when that request went out.
+  /// The runtime clears the inactive signal as the last step of answering, but
+  /// the CP can observe the clear before the published fields land, so a clear
+  /// signal alone does not mean answered. A changed geometry does.
+  uint64_t scratch_published_at_request = 0;
   std::deque<DispatchEntry> entries;
   /// @brief Total entries accepted by this queue, for tests.
   /// @details A replica's entry list is otherwise unobservable after its packets
